34
00:05:22.547 --> 00:05:24.667
little bit of background on that. This dates back to

35
00:05:24.667 --> 00:05:27.507
February of 2023, where we had a request from council.

36
00:05:29.078 --> 00:05:32.438
To investigate the expansion of our automated speed enforcement program

37
00:05:32.438 --> 00:05:36.838
to include community safety zones, including to the entire region.

38
00:05:36.838 --> 00:05:40.118
So we've taken a little bit of time to kind

39
00:05:40.118 --> 00:05:44.449
of put together a response to this request. Next slide,

40
00:05:44.449 --> 00:05:49.938
please. So what are community safety zones? Well, they're defined

41
00:05:49.938 --> 00:05:52.578
within the Highway Traffic act as follows, and that is

42
00:05:52.578 --> 00:05:56.058
that the councillor of a municipality made by bylaw designate

43
00:05:56.058 --> 00:05:59.018
a part of a highway under its jurisdiction as a

44
00:05:59.018 --> 00:06:02.738
community safety zone. If, in his council's opinion, public safety

45
00:06:02.738 --> 00:06:05.578
is of special concern on that, part of the highway.

46
00:06:05.578 --> 00:06:08.098
So that's the framework around what is a community safety

47
00:06:08.098 --> 00:06:10.397
zone. We'll talk a little bit more about what a

48
00:06:10.397 --> 00:06:14.237
community safety zone looks like. So the first part is

49
00:06:14.237 --> 00:06:18.717
around the notion of a regionwide community safety zone, and

50
00:06:18.717 --> 00:06:21.837
our conclusion is that we advise against this. And there's

51
00:06:21.837 --> 00:06:25.277
a few reasons for that. Firstly, it doesn't really comply

52
00:06:25.277 --> 00:06:29.037
with the HTA definition, particularly the part of a highway.

53
00:06:29.924 --> 00:06:33.040
Section. Or where public safety is a special concern. So

54
00:06:33.040 --> 00:06:35.760
it's really intended to be on particular parts of the

55
00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:40.343
highways. It also risks diminishing a public safety tool by

56
00:06:40.343 --> 00:06:43.983
losing its intended effect. Essentially, if everywhere is a community

57
00:06:43.983 --> 00:06:47.303
safety zone than is anywhere a community safety zone. And

58
00:06:47.303 --> 00:06:52.383
third. It has proven historically to be ineffective without supplementary

59
00:06:52.383 --> 00:06:56.981
measures such as physical roadway changes. Or automated speed enforcement

60
00:06:56.981 --> 00:07:01.394
or some kind of enhanced enforcement. And this essentially results

61
00:07:01.394 --> 00:07:06.403
in additional resource pressure on Waterloo region police services. So

62
00:07:06.403 --> 00:07:10.603
instead of a regionwide community safety zone, our recommendation is

63
00:07:10.603 --> 00:07:15.373
that. We designate community safety zones only in priority locations

64
00:07:15.373 --> 00:07:20.001
around the region. Next slide. So what do priority locations

65
00:07:20.001 --> 00:07:25.074
look like? Well. We actually engaged a consulting firm to

66
00:07:25.074 --> 00:07:30.201
help. Us determine. This. And the reason for it is

67
00:07:30.201 --> 00:07:32.081
because there's a lot happening around the province, and we

68
00:07:32.081 --> 00:07:35.926
wanted to make sure. We're kind of operating somewhat in

69
00:07:35.926 --> 00:07:39.110
lockstep with what we're seeing around. The province. So this

70
00:07:39.110 --> 00:07:42.350
work included three major steps. The first was to review

71
00:07:42.350 --> 00:07:44.790
the best practices and the strategies. That are adopted around

72
00:07:44.790 --> 00:07:49.514
the province, including some of our neighboring municipalities. Then from

73
00:07:49.514 --> 00:07:52.154
that, we developed a warrant that was suitable for our

74
00:07:52.154 --> 00:07:55.514
roadways, so kind of tailored. To the region in certain

75
00:07:55.514 --> 00:07:58.114
ways. And then the third step was to kind of

76
00:07:58.114 --> 00:08:02.139
test. That warrant against a number of select locations to

77
00:08:02.139 --> 00:08:04.339
make sure it makes sense, make sure everything's. Not screening

78
00:08:04.339 --> 00:08:09.143
in and that everything isn't screening out. Next slide, please.

79
00:08:09.143 --> 00:08:13.692
So from that work. We ultimately developed, and we're recommending

80
00:08:13.692 --> 00:08:17.292
that staff adopt a two step region of Waterloo. Community

81
00:08:17.292 --> 00:08:20.452
safety zone warrant. So those two steps are, firstly, an

82
00:08:20.452 --> 00:08:26.172
eligibility assessment and second, a points based risk factor assessment.

83
00:08:26.172 --> 00:08:30.455
Next slide. So the eligibility assessment. So this is sort

84
00:08:30.455 --> 00:08:33.335
of the prescreening part of it. What we'll do is

85
00:08:33.335 --> 00:08:34.935
we'll look at a location and we'll look at the

86
00:08:34.935 --> 00:08:39.403
adjacent land uses and check that they're pedestrian focus. So,

87
00:08:39.403 --> 00:08:43.792
is there an elementary or high school? Parks, playgrounds, community

88
00:08:43.792 --> 00:08:50.830
centers, hospitals, daycare centers. Senior centers and senior residences. And

89
00:08:50.830 --> 00:08:53.190
more than just adjacent. We're looking at desire lines, so

90
00:08:53.190 --> 00:08:55.510
we're going to be looking at. Do these land uses

91
00:08:55.510 --> 00:08:58.230
result in a lot of pedestrians that want to cross

92
00:08:58.230 --> 00:09:02.406
or be in close proximity to a regional roadway. So

93
00:09:02.406 --> 00:09:04.606
it allows a little bit of flexibility in terms of

94
00:09:04.606 --> 00:09:09.288
that adjacent land use. We think it would be eligible

95
00:09:09.288 --> 00:09:12.888
on roadways where there is 60 km/hour or less. Speed

96
00:09:12.888 --> 00:09:16.644
limits. And we have a record of poor speed compliance

97
00:09:16.644 --> 00:09:20.328
on those roads. It should be used when there's an

98
00:09:20.328 --> 00:09:23.328
appropriate speed limit in place and no other traffic. Coming

99
00:09:23.328 --> 00:09:28.234
measures present or plan. So that's kind of critical. We

100
00:09:28.234 --> 00:09:32.776
use automated speed enforcement. Generally, when we don't have a

101
00:09:32.776 --> 00:09:36.983
better tool. Available to us immediately to ensure that we

102
00:09:36.983 --> 00:09:39.583
get the low speeds that we're trying to achieve in

103
00:09:39.583 --> 00:09:43.063
that location. So if there are plans for other traffic

104
00:09:43.063 --> 00:09:45.743
coming measures such as a road narrowing or any sort

105
00:09:45.743 --> 00:09:48.543
of measure that might result in lowering speeds than a

106
00:09:48.543 --> 00:09:52.941
community safety zone. Probably isn't the right tool. And lastly,

107
00:09:52.941 --> 00:09:55.461
we want that space to be physically capable to receive

108
00:09:55.461 --> 00:09:58.581
a municipal speed camera. There are physical limitations in terms

109
00:09:58.581 --> 00:10:01.861
of curvature of the roadway and things like that, trees,

110
00:10:01.861 --> 00:10:03.661
things that might limit us from being able to use

111
00:10:03.661 --> 00:10:08.912
a speed camera. Next slide, please. So then if it

112
00:10:08.912 --> 00:10:11.792
passes the eligibility criteria, we move into the step two,

113
00:10:11.792 --> 00:10:14.952
which is the points. Based risk factor assessment. And essentially,

114
00:10:14.952 --> 00:10:19.809
there's eight different risk factors that. We're going to quantify.

115
00:10:21.559 --> 00:10:25.319
Things like the daily traffic volumes, the pedestrian volumes, the

116
00:10:25.319 --> 00:10:28.554
presence. Of adjacent land uses that are going to generate

117
00:10:28.554 --> 00:10:33.490
pedestrian activity. Are there a lot of heavy trucks. Do

118
00:10:33.490 --> 00:10:37.657
we have issues with traffic speeds in the area? Is

119
00:10:37.657 --> 00:10:42.017
there a history of collisions? Is there a high frequency

120
00:10:42.017 --> 00:10:45.577
of intersections and accesses, and is there a presence of

121
00:10:45.577 --> 00:10:50.177
sidewalks? So for each of these. For example, daily traffic

122
00:10:50.177 --> 00:10:52.577
vaughns more traffic equals higher risk. So we assess a

123
00:10:52.577 --> 00:10:57.173
risk factor to each of these factors. For any location

124
00:10:57.173 --> 00:11:02.524
that scores 70% or higher. Than we would deem it

125
00:11:02.524 --> 00:11:09.390
warranted for potentially community safety zone. Next slide. So how

126
00:11:09.390 --> 00:11:15.296
do community safety zones and municipal speed cameras correlate? Well,

127
00:11:15.296 --> 00:11:17.616
given our history, we have a long history of community

128
00:11:17.616 --> 00:11:19.256
safety zones in the province. They date back to the

129
00:11:19.256 --> 00:11:21.776
late 1990s and what we observed in the region and

130
00:11:21.776 --> 00:11:25.716
what's been observed. Generally around the province is that. They're

131
00:11:25.716 --> 00:11:28.956
largely not effective without. Some sort of enforcement or something.

132
00:11:31.032 --> 00:11:34.848
To really. Help achieve the goals of that community safety

133
00:11:34.848 --> 00:11:39.235
zone. And so to that point, Our recommendation is that

134
00:11:39.235 --> 00:11:42.515
community safety zones are always linked to municipal speed cameras

135
00:11:42.515 --> 00:11:45.995
and that further the community safety zone is implemented in

136
00:11:45.995 --> 00:11:48.315
parallel with and around the same time as the activation

137
00:11:48.315 --> 00:11:52.385
of that municipal speed camera. And the reason for that.

138
00:11:52.385 --> 00:11:55.465
As I mentioned, we've had issues in the past, but

139
00:11:55.465 --> 00:12:00.546
we also think it's important to have consistency around. Our

140
00:12:00.546 --> 00:12:02.546
network. So if you see a community safety zone, you

141
00:12:02.546 --> 00:12:05.866
know that that's a critical section of the roadway, and

142
00:12:05.866 --> 00:12:07.946
we're going to be enforcing it with a municipal speed

143
00:12:07.946 --> 00:12:11.346
camera. Because that's the most effective way to ensure that

144
00:12:11.346 --> 00:12:15.194
we achieve the goal so that community safety zone. Next

145
00:12:15.194 --> 00:12:18.307
slide, please. So I'm going to pause for 1 second

146
00:12:18.307 --> 00:12:21.147
here and talk about the municipal speed cameras. And the

147
00:12:21.147 --> 00:12:25.951
program name. We've done some work. A group of us

148
00:12:25.951 --> 00:12:29.511
have done some work to think about the name of

149
00:12:29.511 --> 00:12:33.591
the program. Historically, we've called this automated speed enforcement, but

150
00:12:33.591 --> 00:12:36.751
moving forward, we've. Adopted the municipal speed camera name, and

151
00:12:36.751 --> 00:12:39.904
there's a few reasons for that. One. It provides kind

152
00:12:39.904 --> 00:12:43.424
of clear and plain language that we hope the public

153
00:12:43.424 --> 00:12:48.224
can understand quite easily. Secondly, it communicates a purpose and

154
00:12:48.224 --> 00:12:51.944
the focus of the intended outcome. So it's about reducing

155
00:12:51.944 --> 00:12:55.816
speeds, safer municipalities. It's not about enforcement. We're trying to

156
00:12:55.816 --> 00:12:58.096
remove a little bit of that heavy handedness in the

157
00:12:58.096 --> 00:13:03.582
program title. Third. It increases familiarity. So you see on

158
00:13:03.582 --> 00:13:06.782
the slide, there's the signage that we have. Out on

159
00:13:06.782 --> 00:13:09.862
the street. That sign is required in the hta, wherever

160
00:13:09.862 --> 00:13:12.902
we use the municipal speed. Cameras. So we're proposing to

161
00:13:12.902 --> 00:13:16.822
just basically match the wording on the signs. So moving.

162
00:13:16.822 --> 00:13:20.778
Forward. When we say municipal speed camera, that's technically interchangeable

163
00:13:20.778 --> 00:13:23.338
with automated speed enforcement, but this. Is the program name

164
00:13:23.338 --> 00:13:28.570
that we're recommending to move forward with. Next slide. So

165
00:13:28.570 --> 00:13:32.813
what about community safety zones and school zones? Well, we

166
00:13:32.813 --> 00:13:36.213
recommend layering in a community safety zone at any school

167
00:13:36.213 --> 00:13:38.613
zone where there is a municipal speed camera in use.

168
00:13:40.379 --> 00:13:42.299
The added benefit of this is this will help us

169
00:13:42.299 --> 00:13:45.139
to determine which of the school zones will actually be

170
00:13:45.139 --> 00:13:48.419
eligible for municipal speed camera because we'd be running each

171
00:13:48.419 --> 00:13:52.179
school zone through the same community safety zone. Warrant analysis

172
00:13:52.179 --> 00:13:54.459
if there's not a speeding problem in a school zone.

173
00:13:54.459 --> 00:13:56.939
Then a camera probably isn't a solution that we should

174
00:13:56.939 --> 00:14:01.927
be pursuing there, so we're. Recommend. Recommending layering in community

175
00:14:01.927 --> 00:14:05.807
safety zones in school zones together. Now, again, this applies

176
00:14:05.807 --> 00:14:10.549
on regional roadways where we have jurisdiction. Next slide, please.

177
00:14:10.549 --> 00:14:16.962
So why do we recommend this layering? Well, partly because

178
00:14:16.962 --> 00:14:20.842
we're trying to achieve safer roads near all schools. So

179
00:14:20.842 --> 00:14:23.602
the highway traffic act limits us in terms of our

180
00:14:23.602 --> 00:14:28.627
designation of school zones. There's very specific limitations. While using

181
00:14:28.627 --> 00:14:31.787
community safety zones will allow us to apply the intended

182
00:14:31.787 --> 00:14:34.507
impact on some regional roads that are near a school,

183
00:14:34.507 --> 00:14:37.187
but we can't actually get over the line with a

184
00:14:37.187 --> 00:14:40.947
school zone. Definition. So community safety zone. And again, in

185
00:14:40.947 --> 00:14:43.707
the spirit of being consistent around our application of the

186
00:14:43.707 --> 00:14:47.912
cameras everywhere. We recommend community safety zones and school zones

187
00:14:47.912 --> 00:14:54.368
in parallel. Also. We're ensuring a consistent and best practice

188
00:14:54.368 --> 00:14:58.122
approach with other municipalities in Ontario. As far as I

189
00:14:58.122 --> 00:15:00.602
know, all the other municipalities in Ontario that are using

190
00:15:00.602 --> 00:15:03.842
municipal speed cameras, even in school zones are using community

191
00:15:03.842 --> 00:15:09.016
safety zones as well. It's just. The way that the

192
00:15:09.016 --> 00:15:11.976
province has largely decided, or the municipalities in this province

193
00:15:11.976 --> 00:15:15.216
have largely decided to use this program. The third reason?

194
00:15:17.811 --> 00:15:20.611
Back. One more side. Yes, sorry. The third reason. With

195
00:15:20.611 --> 00:15:24.131
the new administrative penalties program that the region will be

196
00:15:24.131 --> 00:15:27.262
bringing online later this year. The speeding fines for our

197
00:15:27.262 --> 00:15:31.675
municipal speed camera program. They're going to be doubled regardless

198
00:15:31.675 --> 00:15:34.555
of where the infraction occurs, meaning whether it's in a

199
00:15:34.555 --> 00:15:37.475
community safe zone or a school zone, and the benefit

200
00:15:37.475 --> 00:15:40.275
of that community safety zone signage is that it includes

201
00:15:40.275 --> 00:15:43.075
that fines increase notice. So we're effectively communicating with the

202
00:15:43.075 --> 00:15:48.869
public. That if. They were to. Receive a traffic infraction

203
00:15:48.869 --> 00:15:51.349
there that it would be subjected to the doubled fines

204
00:15:51.349 --> 00:15:55.434
later in the Hta. And then the fourth reason. If

205
00:15:55.434 --> 00:15:58.434
the municipal speed camera program expands beyond school areas in

206
00:15:58.434 --> 00:16:02.170
the future. We feel it's important that the sites all

207
00:16:02.170 --> 00:16:08.918
look the same for efficient. And consistent operation. Next slide,

208
00:16:08.918 --> 00:16:12.859
please. And next slide. So just the summary of the

209
00:16:12.859 --> 00:16:16.920
recommendations. So there's four recommendations coming into this report at

210
00:16:16.920 --> 00:16:19.160
this time. The first one is to adopt the two

211
00:16:19.160 --> 00:16:22.320
step region of Waterloo community safety zone warrant as outlined

212
00:16:22.320 --> 00:16:25.768
in this report. The second is to direct staff to

213
00:16:25.768 --> 00:16:29.048
identify which regional school zones should have community safety zone

214
00:16:29.048 --> 00:16:32.608
designation. Added in parallel with the municipal speed camera changes

215
00:16:32.608 --> 00:16:37.126
planned for late 2024. The third is to adopt the

216
00:16:37.126 --> 00:16:40.606
strategy of using a community safety zone designation at all

217
00:16:40.606 --> 00:16:44.606
school zones. Along regional roadways that are to be equipped

218
00:16:44.606 --> 00:16:48.726
with municipal speed cameras in the future and the fourth

219
00:16:48.726 --> 00:16:51.366
is to direct staff to use the newly adopted community

220
00:16:51.366 --> 00:16:55.855
safety zone warrant to identify a comprehensive list. Of prospective

221
00:16:55.855 --> 00:16:59.255
community safety zones along regional roads, including those not centered

222
00:16:59.255 --> 00:17:02.255
around a school zone, and report back to Councillor with

223
00:17:02.255 --> 00:17:06.615
options for implementing those community safety zones, including associated impacts

224
00:17:06.615 --> 00:17:09.655
to the staffing. And resources for the region's municipal speed

225
00:17:09.655 --> 00:17:14.531
camera program. So for that fourth recommendation, we anticipate it'll

226
00:17:14.531 --> 00:17:18.091
take six to nine months to. Identify comprehensive list of

227
00:17:18.091 --> 00:17:20.491
community safety zones. As you can imagine, that's a rather

228
00:17:20.491 --> 00:17:23.651
data intensive exercises. We need to check speeds and land

229
00:17:23.651 --> 00:17:29.903
uses throughout our network. Next slide, please. So in terms

230
00:17:29.903 --> 00:17:34.063
of the implications for the area municipalities, the recommendations in

231
00:17:34.063 --> 00:17:38.663
our report, they apply for regional roadways only. Essentially, the

232
00:17:38.663 --> 00:17:44.439
roadways where we have jurisdiction. It's our opinion that consistency

233
00:17:44.439 --> 00:17:47.999
across all roadways in the region, regardless of the jurisdiction.

234
00:17:47.999 --> 00:17:50.079
Would be best for the integrity of the region's municipal

235
00:17:50.079 --> 00:17:55.120
